`@micacms/astro` connects the model to Astro's content layer. It reads the files during the build and needs no server, no token and no network.

## micaCollections(site, options)

```ts
// src/content.config.ts
import { micaCollections } from "@micacms/astro";
import { site } from "./mica";

export const collections = micaCollections(site);
```

It gives one Astro collection for each collection and for each singleton of the model. A singleton is a collection with one entry, whose id is the singleton's name. The build also writes the model to `<content root>/.mica/schema.json`. Commit that file: the editor reads the model from it. In CI (when the environment variable `CI` is set), the build does not write the file. It fails when the committed file is not the current model.

| Option | Type | Default | Effect |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `root` | `string` | `defineSite({ root })`, else `./content` | The content root. A relative path starts at the Astro project. Use an absolute path for content in a different checkout. |
| `publishModel` | `false` | on | `false` does not write `.mica/schema.json`. Use it only for a project that nobody edits with Mica. |
| `debounceMs` | `number` | `100` | In `astro dev`, the wait after a file change before Mica reloads. One save changes several files. |
| `strict` | `boolean` | `false` | Stops the build at a relation to a missing entry or at a missing media file. Without it, the build shows a warning. A missing image stops the build in all cases. |

You can put ordinary Astro collections next to the Mica collections:

```ts
export const collections = { ...micaCollections(site), glossary };
```

For a reference set of the model, the loader also writes its index to `<content root>/.mica/references/<name>.json`. It writes it again in `astro dev` when the file changes. In CI, the build fails when the committed index is not the one of the file. See [Reference data](/docs/reference-data/).

## The data of an entry

`entry.id` is the slug, which is also the folder or file name in the repository. Each entry of a collection has `data._mica`. It holds two keys:

| Key | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `position` | The place in the collection's order, the order that the editor shows: the `defaultSort` of the collection, else the order in `_order.json` when there is one, then the other slugs in the order of their characters (UTF-16 code units), the same on every computer. |
| `images` | The images of the entry's rich text whose files are in the content, as `{ path, src }`: the path as the text writes it, and the image that Astro made. A singleton has `data._mica.images` too. `renderRichText` reads them, so you rarely use them yourself. |

`_mica` has no `id`, `slug` or `collection`. Use `entry.id` and the name of the collection. There are no dates in `_mica`, because in Git a date is commit data. Add a `date()` field when editors must see or set a date.

## Helpers for pages

| Helper | Gives |
| --- | --- |
| `inEditorOrder` | A function for `sort`: it puts the entries in the order that the editor shows. `(await getCollection("team")).sort(inEditorOrder)` |
| `getSingleton(site, name)` | The data of a singleton, typed by the model. It stops the build with a clear message when nobody saved the singleton yet. |
| `mediaFile(site, path)` | The file on disk for a media path, to read a GPX track or another file at build time. A `..` in the path is refused. |

See [Recipes](/docs/recipes/) for each one in use.

## Media

| Field | Value in Astro |
| --- | --- |
| `media({ accept: "image" })` | An image (`ImageMetadata`), ready for `<Image src={...} />`. |
| `media({ accept: "image", alt: "required" })` or `"optional"` | `{ src, alt }`, so `<Image {...entry.data.cover} />` is enough. A missing optional alt text is `""`. |
| `media()` with another `accept` | The path of the file from the content root, as a string, or `{ path, alt }` with `alt`. Astro does not copy such a file. Read it with `mediaFile`, or copy it in an endpoint. |
| `multiple: true` | A list of the values above. |

## Relations

A relation stores the slug of the target entry, which is also its id in Astro. `@micacms/astro/relations` gives two functions for your pages.

```ts
import { related, relatedAll } from "@micacms/astro/relations";

const author = await related("team", article.data.author);
const people = await relatedAll("team", project.data.team);
```

- `related(collection, slug)` gives the entry, or `undefined` when the field is empty or the entry does not exist.
- `relatedAll(collection, slugs)` gives the entries in the order that the editor chose, and leaves out the entries that do not exist.

In `astro dev`, a missing entry shows a warning. Use them only in pages and components: they import `astro:content`. They also read the items of a reference set, because it is an Astro collection.

## Rich text

A `richText()` field is Markdoc, in a `.mdoc` file beside the entry. The functions run during the build.

| Function | Gives |
| --- | --- |
| `await renderRichText(source, entry.data, options?)` | An HTML string. An empty source gives `""`. It is async. |
| `await transformRichText(source, entry.data, options?)` | Markdoc's render tree, to render with your own components. |
| `validateRichText(source, options?)` | Markdoc's list of problems, for a strict build. `renderRichText` does not stop at a problem. |

The second argument is the data of the entry or the singleton. `renderRichText` finds its images in `_mica.images`, and Astro's image service makes them smaller: a `srcset` and `sizes` in 480, 800, 1200 and 1600 px as WebP, up to the width of the original. The site needs `sharp`, as for `<Image>`. An image with a `/path` that is not in the content, for example one in `public/`, keeps its path.

| Option | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `config` | A Markdoc configuration. Add your own tags there; a tag that the configuration does not declare gives no output. |
| `optimize` | `false` writes the original files with their width and height. An object sets `widths`, `sizes` and `format` (`"webp"`, `"avif"`, `"png"` or `"jpeg"`). |

Markdoc refuses `javascript:` and `data:` links in its own nodes, but not in a tag or node that you add: check every URL attribute of your own tags, so that an editor cannot add a script link. Raw HTML in the source is not rendered. See [Recipes](/docs/recipes/) for a custom tag.

## Maps: @micacms/astro/components

`LocationMap` draws the value of a `coordinates()` field with MapLibre. It has no styles of its own: give it a size with `class` or `style`.

```astro
---
import { LocationMap } from "@micacms/astro/components";
---
<LocationMap place={venue.place} map="positron" label="Map: Town hall" class="block h-56 w-full" />
```

| Prop | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `place` | The value of the field: `{ lat, lng }`, with `zoom` when the editor saved one. |
| `map` | The field's `map` option, or another style. Default: `"liberty"`. |
| `zoom` | The zoom when the value stores none. Default: 14. |
| `label` | The name of the map for a screen reader. |
| `linkText` | The text of the link that stands in for the map. |

The website needs `maplibre-gl` in its dependencies. The map loads when it comes near the screen. Until then, and without JavaScript, the element holds a link to the place on OpenStreetMap. `mapView(place, { map, zoom })` from `@micacms/astro` gives the same view as data, for a map that you draw yourself.

## Live preview: @micacms/astro/preview

`micaPreview()` lets the local editor's preview find the texts of a page. A click on a text in the preview opens its field, and the preview scrolls to the field that the editor works in.

```js
// astro.config.mjs
import { micaPreview } from "@micacms/astro/preview";

export default defineConfig({ integrations: [micaPreview()] });
```

Start the dev server with `MICA_PREVIEW=1 astro dev`. Only then does the integration do something. A build and a plain `astro dev` do not change. The command sets the variable in a POSIX shell only. For a package script that also runs on Windows, use `cross-env`:

```json
{
  "scripts": {
    "dev:preview": "cross-env MICA_PREVIEW=1 astro dev"
  }
}
```

- The loaders append invisible characters to the values of text and textarea fields. Values that look like an address or a phone number get none, because a template puts them in a link. In the preview, a template that compares a text value or makes an address from it can act differently.
- Each page gets a small script. It answers only an editor on the same computer. To allow another editor, set `MICA_PREVIEW_EDITOR` to its origins, separated by commas.

See [Preview changes](/docs/previews/).

## Types

```ts
import type { CollectionDocument, SingletonDocument } from "@micacms/astro";
import type { site } from "./mica";

type Project = CollectionDocument<typeof site, "projects">;
type Settings = SingletonDocument<typeof site, "settings">;
```

`getCollection` and `getEntry` also give these types, because the schema comes from the model.
